# Undo Staged Change Notes

Used when **changes are added (staged) but not yet committed**.

---

## Git Reset

- **git reset <filename>** → Unstages a specific file, keeping its changes in your working directory.  
- **git reset** → Unstages all staged files, keeping changes in your working directory.  

---

## Modern Alternative

- **git restore --staged <filename>** → Modern way to unstage a file.  
- Performs the same action as `git reset <filename>` but is considered more **explicit and safer**.  

 # Undo Committed Change Notes

Used when **changes are already committed** but you want to undo them.

---

## Git Reset Commands

- **git reset HEAD~1** → Undo the last commit **but keeps changes** in your working directory.  
  - *Soft/Mixed reset*: Safe undo, allows editing and recommitting.  

- **git reset --hard HEAD~1** → Undo the last commit **and deletes all changes** made in that commit.  
  - *Hard reset*: Permanent removal of commit and changes. Use with caution.  

---

## Quick Warning

- Both commands move the **tip of your branch back by one commit**.  
- Choose **soft/mixed** to save your work, **hard** only to wipe everything completely.  

# Undo Committed Changes (Jump to Specific Commit)

Used when you want to **go back to a specific commit** in your Git history.

---

## Git Reset Commands

- **git reset <commit-id>** → Move the branch to a specific commit, keeping changes from later commits as **unstaged**.  
  - Safe way to go back and edit/recommit changes.

- **git reset --hard <commit-id>** → Move the branch to a specific commit and **delete all changes** after it permanently.  
  - Dangerous: No way to undo once executed.

---

## How It Works

- `<commit-id>` is the **SHA-1 hash** of the commit you want to return to.  
- Git restores your project to the **exact snapshot** of that commit.  
- Use soft reset to save work, hard reset to fully wipe unwanted commits.

# Git Log --oneline Notes

The `git log --oneline` command shows a **condensed view of your commit history**.  

- Each line = **one commit**  
- Format: **Commit ID + Commit Message**  

---

## Example Terminal Output

| Commit ID | Branch/Pointer Info | Commit Message |
|-----------|-------------------|----------------|
| 7d5493e | (HEAD -> main) | new line added |
| 2db8f08 | — | Changes by Tariq Mahoom |
| 2df18a3 | (origin/function1) | Changes made in html |
| b0ba3ec | — | Readme file created |
| 8db439a | — | CSS File Created |
| 3d0ad17 | — | New HTML file created |

---

## Key Notes

- **Commit ID:** Unique 7-character alphanumeric hash identifying the commit.  
- **Branch/Pointer Info:** Shows current branch (`HEAD`) and remote tracking branch info.  
- **Commit Message:** Short description of what was changed.  

✅ Tip: Use `git log --oneline` for a **quick overview** of your project history.